Entropy formation distinguishes a whole distribution, a particular macrostate and the grouped entropy decomposition. The block connects energy-sharing maxima to entropy slopes and stability conditions, retains the earlier thermodynamic second-law premises, and does not derive a relaxation law from counting. Finite reservoir weights precede the canonical approximation; a finite Taylor remainder controls probability ratios only on a common valid support. Canonical differentiation, fixed-level heat capacity, entropy/Helmholtz identities and the finite relative-entropy minimum are derived with their conditions. Correlation counterexamples and concentration bounds distinguish model fluctuation from measurement uncertainty and sample inference.

